\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Charge current for $P=\frac  {1}{2}$, $V=\SI {0.5}{\volt }$, varying $n_\mathrm  {vib}$ for tolerances tol$\ = 10^{-3}$ (purple, green), and tol$\ = 10^{-4}$ (blue, yellow) (top and middle panel); comparison of charge currents for $A=\frac  {1}{2}$ and $A=-\frac  {1}{2}$ (bottom panel). This plot shows that for a higher tolerance, it appears that the vibrational basis is not converged. This is not true, because when decreasing the error tolerance, no difference for $n_\mathrm  {vib}=20$ and $n_\mathrm  {vib}=80$ is observed. The difference in charge currents for opposing lead magnetizations (for a low tolerance) is as large as the change in the current when decreasing the tolerance.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
